.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.env
node_modules/
frontend/.next/
frontend/out/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.DS_Store

# Runner output: span trees. Regenerable OFFLINE from the committed cassettes with
# `make spans` (MOCK_LLM=1, no GPU, no network), so committing 42 of them would be
# committing a derived artifact that anyone can rebuild byte-identically.
out/

# Spike raw output: recorded provider/model responses. Regenerable, and a failed
# self-test run is not evidence of anything. The written finding lands in
# docs/spikes/*.md and the ADRs -- that is what gets committed.
docs/spikes/*-raw/

# EXCEPT S4. Found by M3-5c's cold run, and the exception is the interesting part: for
# every other spike the raw output IS a self-test, and the rule above is right. S4's
# records are not that. They are the MEASUREMENT INPUT that scripts/build_faithfulness_panel.py
# reads to produce faithfulness/panel.json -- the committed artifact behind B4 #6's
# published "0 of 48 trials flipped", the negative result ADR-009 rests on.
#
# Two things followed from ignoring them, both bad. `make faithfulness-check` -- the
# command E10's evidence line cites as CI's defence against the panel drifting from its
# source -- could not run on any machine but the author's, so the check that exists to be
# verified was the one thing nobody could verify. And a published headline had its
# denominator on one laptop, regenerable only by spending money against a model that may
# no longer return what it returned in September. 40 KB is a cheap price for a number
# somebody can check.
!docs/spikes/S4-raw/
!docs/spikes/S4-raw/**
